WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:08.323
Yan Chen: Okay, I think everyone's here. David, you asked to record, I gave you permission. Go ahead.

2
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:15.752
David Barron: Thanks. Recording's on. It's mostly so Yue can catch up, since she's dialing in from the airport later.

3
00:00:17.115 --> 00:00:26.531
Yan Chen: Good. So, we have one day. The rebuttal goes up tomorrow, the twenty-ninth. I want to leave today with every response assigned and the numbers final.

4
00:00:26.835 --> 00:00:32.091
Yan Chen: David, can you start with the leakage issue, so everyone has the same picture?

5
00:00:33.518 --> 00:00:43.313
David Barron: Yeah. So, on Friday, the twenty-fourth, I was doing per-student error analysis for the reviewer who asked about failure cases, and the counts didn't add up.

6
00:00:43.386 --> 00:00:48.140
David Barron: Some students had windows in the test set and also in train.

7
00:00:48.688 --> 00:00:53.781
David Barron: The split script was splitting at the window level, not the student level.

8
00:00:54.166 --> 00:01:00.184
David Barron: So thirty-second windows from the same student, sometimes from the same session, were on both sides.

9
00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:05.865
David Barron: The model could basically learn a student's typing rhythm and recognize them in test.

10
00:01:06.406 --> 00:01:11.277
Xiaohang: And it wasn't a few students, right? It was most of them.

11
00:01:12.115 --> 00:01:20.081
David Barron: It was most of them. Around ninety percent of test students also had windows in train. So it's not a corner case.

12
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.465
Yan Chen: And the fix?

13
00:01:23.593 --> 00:01:31.984
David Barron: The fix is a split grouped by student id. Every student is entirely in train, entirely in validation, or entirely in test.

14
00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:39.888
David Barron: Same proportions as before, seventy, ten, twenty, by student instead of by window. I reran the main model over the weekend.

15
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.607
Yan Chen: And the number.

16
00:01:42.342 --> 00:01:46.209
David Barron: Macro-F1 goes from point eight one to point seven four.

17
00:01:47.743 --> 00:01:49.220
Yan Chen: Okay. Seven points.

18
00:01:50.616 --> 00:01:59.046
David Barron: Yeah. It hurts but it's, um, it's the real number. The per-class drop is mostly on the stalled class and the help-seeking class.

19
00:01:59.248 --> 00:02:01.608
David Barron: Productive and off-task barely move.

20
00:02:10.687 --> 00:02:13.043
Minsun: That actually makes sense to me.

21
00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:24.671
Minsun: Stalled windows are the ones where the student does the same thing over and over, so if the model has seen that student before it can just memorize their pattern.

22
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.737
David Barron: Exactly, yeah.

23
00:02:28.497 --> 00:02:34.677
Yan Chen: So my position, and I want to check everyone agrees: we report both numbers in the rebuttal.

24
00:02:34.898 --> 00:02:47.033
Yan Chen: We say clearly that we found a split error after submission, we describe it, we give the corrected number, and we say the revised paper and the released splits use the grouped split.

25
00:02:47.491 --> 00:02:49.909
Yan Chen: No hiding it in a footnote.

26
00:02:51.052 --> 00:02:54.883
Rezky: Agree. If a reviewer finds it later it's much worse.

27
00:02:56.199 --> 00:03:00.232
Xiaohang: Agree. And honestly point seven four is still above every baseline.

28
00:03:01.603 --> 00:03:04.106
Yan Chen: Is it? With the new split?

29
00:03:05.353 --> 00:03:11.252
Xiaohang: That's the thing I need to confirm. I reran logistic regression and the GRU yesterday.

30
00:03:11.618 --> 00:03:19.565
Xiaohang: Logistic regression goes from point six six to point six one, the GRU from point seven three to point six eight.

31
00:03:19.978 --> 00:03:24.844
Xiaohang: I haven't rerun the CodeBERT baseline yet, it takes about six hours.

32
00:03:25.922 --> 00:03:28.343
Yan Chen: Can you have it by tonight?

33
00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:35.903
Xiaohang: If I start it right after this, yes. I'll have it by six or seven.

34
00:03:36.765 --> 00:03:43.425
Yan Chen: Good. Then the table in the general response has old and new numbers side by side, every row.

35
00:03:43.695 --> 00:03:47.459
Yan Chen: David, you write the general response, including the leakage paragraph.

36
00:03:47.958 --> 00:03:57.439
David Barron: Yep. I already have a draft of the leakage paragraph, it's about a hundred and fifty words. I'll put it in the doc after this call.

37
00:03:58.889 --> 00:04:06.464
Yan Chen: Keep it plain. What happened, what we changed, what the new numbers are, what we release. Don't apologize three times.

38
00:04:07.528 --> 00:04:09.200
David Barron: Ha. Okay. One apology.

39
00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:16.604
Yan Chen: Now the individual reviewers. Reviewer one, the baselines. That's David too, since it's mostly the new table.

40
00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.677
David Barron: Okay. Reviewer one also asked whether we tried a non-neural sequence model.

41
00:04:22.138 --> 00:04:31.963
David Barron: I think we can say the GRU covers sequence and logistic regression covers the non-neural side, and offer an HMM in the revision if they want it.

42
00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:37.585
Yan Chen: Don't offer things we won't do. Just answer what they asked.

43
00:04:38.764 --> 00:04:39.864
David Barron: Fair. Okay.

44
00:04:40.638 --> 00:04:46.155
Yan Chen: Reviewer two, label quality. Minsun, that's yours. They want agreement statistics and the taxonomy justification.

45
00:04:51.113 --> 00:04:59.874
Minsun: Yes. I have the agreement on the four-hundred-window overlap set, Krippendorff's alpha was point six eight overall. I can break it down per class.

46
00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:04.211
Minsun: Stalled versus help-seeking is the pair with the most disagreement.

47
00:05:05.499 --> 00:05:09.305
Yan Chen: Say that. And say what the adjudication process was.

48
00:05:10.684 --> 00:05:19.607
Minsun: Okay. I'll also add one sentence on how the taxonomy came from the VizPI labeling guide, since that's where the stalled-after-error state came from.

49
00:05:20.538 --> 00:05:23.405
Yan Chen: Good. Reviewer three: privacy, consent, license. Rezky.

50
00:05:26.863 --> 00:05:33.979
Rezky: Yes. So they asked three things. Was there IRB approval, how was consent collected, and what is the license.

51
00:05:34.347 --> 00:05:40.370
Rezky: IRB, yes, I have the protocol number, I'll just cite that it was approved and exempt-reviewed.

52
00:05:40.501 --> 00:05:47.040
Rezky: Consent was opt-in at the start of the semester, students could withdraw any time and twelve did.

53
00:05:47.103 --> 00:05:52.857
Rezky: License is CC BY-NC 4.0 with a data use agreement for the raw keystroke files.

54
00:05:53.873 --> 00:05:54.973
Yan Chen: And de-identification.

55
00:05:55.828 --> 00:06:04.575
Rezky: Right. Student ids are hashed with a salt we don't release, and we strip file paths and any string literals that match name patterns.

56
00:06:04.719 --> 00:06:07.110
Rezky: I'll describe that in two sentences.

57
00:06:08.795 --> 00:06:14.678
Yan Chen: Good. Reviewer four, generalization. Yue's not here yet. Does anyone know where she is with it?

58
00:06:16.064 --> 00:06:24.223
Minsun: She messaged me. She has the per-course breakdown, CS1 versus CS2, and she's joining in a few minutes, she's at the gate.

59
00:06:24.842 --> 00:06:29.475
Yue: Hi, sorry, I'm here. Can you hear me? It's very loud here.

60
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.910
Yan Chen: We can hear you. Yue, the generalization response, where are you?

61
00:06:41.732 --> 00:06:50.494
Yue: Yes, so, um, I have the per-course numbers but they were on the old split. I need David's new split file to redo them.

62
00:06:51.988 --> 00:07:00.024
David Barron: It's on the lab drive, and the script is in the PR, the one called grouped split. The output is split_by_student.json.

63
00:07:01.674 --> 00:07:08.962
Yue: Okay, I'll rerun on the plane, I have the data locally. Should I report train on CS1, test on CS2?

64
00:07:10.109 --> 00:07:17.704
Yan Chen: Yes, that's the one they asked for. Train on one course, test on the other, both directions if you can.

65
00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:25.452
Yue: Okay. Both directions. I'll put the numbers in the doc before I land, so maybe five p.m.

66
00:07:25.989 --> 00:07:32.894
Yan Chen: Good. Now, timing. Everyone's draft in the shared doc by six tonight. I'll do a pass at nine.

67
00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:37.395
Yan Chen: David, you check the character limits, it's five thousand per response.

68
00:07:38.638 --> 00:07:44.320
David Barron: Yep. I'll do the counting and the formatting. Do we post tonight or tomorrow morning?

69
00:07:45.167 --> 00:07:50.583
Yan Chen: Tomorrow morning. Nobody posts anything without me reading it. We post by noon tomorrow.

70
00:07:52.014 --> 00:07:58.436
Xiaohang: One question. Should we update the numbers in the paper PDF now, or only in the rebuttal?

71
00:07:59.693 --> 00:08:09.153
Yan Chen: Only the rebuttal for now. We say the revised version will use the grouped split everywhere. If they allow a revised PDF, we do it then.

72
00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:11.040
Xiaohang: Okay.

73
00:08:12.319 --> 00:08:16.599
Minsun: Should we also mention it in the datasheet, the split issue?

74
00:08:17.733 --> 00:08:23.216
Rezky: I can add a line to the datasheet changelog. Version one point one, grouped splits.

75
00:08:24.829 --> 00:08:27.141
Yan Chen: Yes, do that. Anything else? David?

76
00:08:31.749 --> 00:08:37.498
David Barron: Just, um, thanks everyone for moving fast on this. I know it's a bad week.

77
